  "body": "Resource and network calls now retain borrowed metadata and release owned files, channels, HTTP/2 and gRPC contexts through errors and cancellation. Typed State scopes own stored values, get/results and replacements, including escaping closures. Synchronous list callbacks and 14 serializer/codec leaves have explicit ownership contracts.\n\nScoped autodiff callbacks release tapes on return and nonlocal exits. Direct borrowed arguments avoid temporary callback closures and restore gradient allocation parity. Effect inference accepts compatible outer callback effects, preserves nested input rows, rejects missing Async/IO and reports original callback types on mismatches. Compiler failure diagnostics, executable-cache lifetime, formatting and roadmap documentation are also repaired. Codec tests preserve the source fixture when copying artifacts to the default directory on macOS.\n\nValidation: final combined preflight38180262247 passes 71 library units, original type snapshots, task/State/AD and codec alias controls, affine File rejection, formatting and stdlib docs. Independent Linux/ARM/Intel profiles and combined-source Linux profiles use complete initializer-aware allocation counters. Scoped gradients match the baseline at 33 managed calls/720 requested bytes per call; State reduces allocations and list callbacks improve counted reclamation. Hardware, compiler, flags and source/binary hashes are retained with the profiles. Both default and explicit codec artifact paths pass on Linux, ARM macOS and Intel macOS in preflight38184029472 with all original alias and GC/reuse checks.\n\nCurrent head3aae99 must pass all six Linux/macOS jobs in38184549852 and roadmap_docs38184549850 before squash. Tracing remains required for unmodeled boundaries and cycles; these workload-specific results establish no timing gain or tracing-free support. The plan and handoff retain the finite remaining ownership review and phases3\u20136.\n",
